1: # The labelled equivariant plumbing has a nonzero determinant obstruction
2: 
3: Working proof for audit. We retain the actual selected-cusp positive
4: rho loop of P3F.54/P3F.56 and its initial fan. The claim here is precise:
5: the neighbourhood of its three labelled complex curves, with their two
6: labelled vertices and positive complex curve orientations, has no
7: periodic smooth product which intertwines the physical delta action
8: and carries all those labelled submanifolds to their constant copies.
9: 
10: This is an obstruction to that particular simultaneous marked product.
11: It does not obstruct the separate products of P3K.40 and OE.2, the
12: actual cap map of P3K.46, or computation of any return by another
13: explicit comparison. We do not assign the two nonfixed relative
14: integers or an all-fan return formula.
15: 
16: ## PO.1. The actual family and the derivative marks
17: 
18: Use the two actual vertex charts \((a,b,c)\) and \((u,v,w)\), with
19: \(q=abc=uvw\). The fixed curve \(C_0\) has tangent coordinates
20: \(b,w\), the other two labelled curves \(C_1,C_2\) have tangent
21: coordinates \((c,v)\) and \((a,u)\), respectively. The actual
22: transition arrows from OE.4 are
23: \[
24:  \begin{array}{c|ccc}
25:  &u&v&w\\ \hline
26:  C_0&\kappa\alpha bc&\alpha^{-1}ab&\kappa^{-1}b^{-1}\\
27:  C_1&g\eta bc&\eta^{-1}c^{-1}&g^{-1}ac\\
28:  C_2&a^{-1}&ab&ac.
29:  \end{array}                                                   \tag{PO.1}
30: \]
31: Only their restrictions and normal derivatives along the curves are
32: used below. Thus all units in the proof mean their restrictions at
33: \(q=0\), as functions of rho. The exact F54/F56 units satisfy
34: \[
35:  \operatorname{wind}\kappa=-6,\qquad
36:  \operatorname{wind}\alpha=\operatorname{wind}\eta=0. \tag{PO.2}
37: \]
38: The nonzero scalar is fixed, and \(\eta=\lambda\beta\). No physical
39: height character is changed to \(q_T\).
40: 
41: Suppose the stated periodic marked product existed. Read it as a map
42: from the actual plumbing over rho to one fixed reference plumbing.
43: At the two vertices its derivatives on the tangent lines of
44: \(C_2,C_1\) have nonzero complex factors
45: \[
46:                A,C\quad\hbox{and}\quad U,V,
47:                                                                \tag{PO.3}
48: \]
49: respectively. These factors are complex linear even though the product
50: is only smooth. Indeed these real two-planes have physical circle
51: weights \(-1,+1\) at the first vertex and \(+1,-1\) at the second;
52: the map intertwines that circle, retains each labelled axis, and
53: retains its complex orientation. The commutant on an individual such
54: real rotation plane is the complex scalar group. Put
55: \[
56:  a_* =\operatorname{wind}A,\quad
57:  c_* =\operatorname{wind}C,\quad
58:  u_* =\operatorname{wind}U,\quad
59:  v_* =\operatorname{wind}V.                            \tag{PO.4}
60: \]
61: All are integers because the product is periodic. We do not assume
62: the derivative stays diagonal along the fixed curve away from the
63: vertices.
64: 
65: ## PO.2. The two curve products force their endpoint winding equations
66: 
67: Here is the elementary pointed-curve argument, with its actual unit
68: retained. For a curve having tangent coordinates \(z_+,z_-\) at its
69: two labelled vertices and transition
70: \(z_-=\xi(\rho)^{-1}/z_+\), write the reference transition with the
71: fixed nonzero constant \(\xi_*\). A marked equivariant product on
72: the open orbit cylinder gives an orientation-preserving equivariant
73: map \(F_\rho:\mathbb C^*\to\mathbb C^*\). The ratio
74: \(F_\rho(z_+)/z_+\) is invariant under the rotation of \(z_+\), so
75: its rho winding is independent of the radius and of the angle of
76: \(z_+\). This remains true on a rho-dependent nonzero path by
77: radially interpolating its modulus to a fixed modulus.
78: 
79: Near the first endpoint that ratio tends to its nonzero derivative
80: factor \(P(\rho)\). At the second endpoint the derivative factor
81: \(Q(\rho)\), in the two displayed endpoint coordinates, gives
82: \[
83:  \frac{F_\rho(z_+)}{z_+}
84:           \longrightarrow\frac{\xi(\rho)}{\xi_*Q(\rho)}.
85: \]
86: Taking winding numbers proves
87: \[
88:                    \operatorname{wind}P+
89:                    \operatorname{wind}Q
90:                          =\operatorname{wind}\xi.       \tag{PO.5}
91: \]
92: The assertion concerns the actual smooth map, not a replacement by
93: a complex automorphism of the curve. Equivariance is enough for the
94: ratio argument; the positive complex orientation fixes its degree
95: on the open orbit cylinder.
96: 
97: For \(C_2\) the unit \(\xi\) is one, while for \(C_1\) it is
98: \(\eta\), by (PO.1). Consequently (PO.5) gives
99: \[
100:                  a_*+u_*=0,\qquad
101:                  c_*+v_* =\operatorname{wind}\eta.     \tag{PO.6}
102: \]
103: 
104: ## PO.3. The full U(2) determinant cannot match these endpoints
105: 
106: On the normal real four-plane to \(C_0\), the infinitesimal physical
107: delta action defines a canonical complex structure: conjugate its
108: negative-weight complex line and keep its positive-weight line. At
109: the first vertex use the ordered complex normal coordinates
110: \((\overline a,c)\); at the second use
111: \((\overline v,u)\). Along the fixed curve any smooth equivariant
112: normal derivative is a complex-linear invertible two-by-two map in
113: this structure. After a positive Hermitian homotopy it can be viewed
114: as U(2), but the argument below uses its nonzero complex determinant
115: directly, without assuming diagonality.
116: 
117: The actual fixed-curve normal transition obtained from (PO.1) is
118: \[
119:  \begin{pmatrix}\overline v\\u\end{pmatrix}
120:  =\begin{pmatrix}
121:        \overline\alpha^{-1}\overline b&0\\
122:        0&\kappa\alpha b
123:    \end{pmatrix}
124:    \begin{pmatrix}\overline a\\c\end{pmatrix}.
125:                                                                \tag{PO.7}
126: \]
127: Its determinant is
128: \[
129:               \kappa\,\frac\alpha{\overline\alpha}\,|b|^2.
130:                                                                \tag{PO.8}
131: \]
132: In the reference plumbing the corresponding determinant has constant
133: phase: its units are their fixed initial values and the source-coordinate
134: factor is the positive real norm square. In particular its phase has
135: zero rho winding, regardless of the smooth source-coordinate map in
136: the assumed product.
137: 
138: Let \(G_+,G_-\) be the normal-derivative matrices of that product,
139: expressed in the two actual normal charts and a nonzero global
140: determinant frame on the reference normal bundle. Such a reference
141: determinant frame exists explicitly: (PO.8) at the fixed reference
142: parameter has constant phase and positive variable factor, so its
143: two chart frames glue after positive rescaling and a constant phase.
144: The winding of \(\det G_+\) on any overlap parameter loop equals
145: its winding at the first vertex. This follows by contracting the
146: point in the first source-coordinate disc to that vertex while
147: keeping the determinant nonzero. The same argument in the second
148: disc applies to \(G_-\), even when its overlap coordinate depends
149: on rho. At the vertices (PO.3) gives, in the stated positive-weight
150: complex structure,
151: \[
152:  \operatorname{wind}\det G_+=-a_*+c_*,\qquad
153:  \operatorname{wind}\det G_-= -v_*+u_* .             \tag{PO.9}
154: \]
155: 
156: The derivative is one actual bundle map. Therefore its two matrices
157: and (PO.7) must commute with the reference normal transition. Taking
158: determinants and winding numbers in this exact square gives
159: \[
160:  (-v_*+u_*)+\operatorname{wind}\kappa
161:              +2\operatorname{wind}\alpha
162:                      -(-a_*+c_*)=0.                   \tag{PO.10}
163: \]
164: This equality holds for arbitrary invertible complex normal matrices
165: between the vertices. Off-diagonal U(2) terms cannot change it.
166: Substituting the two actual endpoint equations (PO.6) leaves
167: \[
168:  \operatorname{wind}\kappa+2\operatorname{wind}\alpha
169:                      -\operatorname{wind}\eta=0.       \tag{PO.11}
170: \]
171: Its left-hand side is \(-6\) by (PO.2). This is the required
172: contradiction. Thus the stated simultaneous marked equivariant
173: product does not exist.
174: 
175: ## PO.4. Exact scope of the obstruction
176: 
177: The obstruction is the relative determinant winding of the actual
178: fixed-curve normal family, with its endpoint frames supplied by the
179: two other labelled pointed curve products. It is already detected
180: by the determinant; a contraction in SU(2) does not alter (PO.10).
181: The earlier even-winding factorization in OE.4 transfers the seam
182: between vertices and retains a physical-circle loop. It does not
183: satisfy both endpoint equations together with the determinant square.
184: 
185: In particular a periodic equivariant product connection on this
186: actual labelled plumbing, with identity return on that entire
187: marked neighbourhood, would give the forbidden product by its
188: parallel transport. This identifies the precise missing hypothesis
189: in an attempted direct application of the scalar-loop simultaneous
190: product argument. It makes no assertion about another global
191: representative whose isotopy moves these labelled curves, or about
192: an all-fan computation retaining the nontrivial boundary clutch.
193: 
194: The separate fixed-edge product and the nonfixed-star products
195: remain valid. The actual half-source reflected-family and cap maps
196: of P3K.45/P3K.46 remain valid as well: they transport one fixed-edge
197: product and do not assert a simultaneous product of the three
198: labelled curve stars.
199: 
200: The exact determinant and integer-consistency certificate is
201: code/part3/source_cusp_product_obstruction.py. No numerical
202: nonfixed relative return is assigned by this proof.
